      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1844 the town adopted the name Murray to honor Representative James Murray, replacing its earlier names of Williston and Pleasant Springs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murray%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Murray State University started as the Murray Normal School in 1922 and now enjoys over 1,300 wins in men’s basketball, ranking 20th in NCAA history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murray%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "More than 170,000 acres of forest at Land Between The Lakes surround Murray, featuring the Golden Pond Planetarium, a 1850s Working Farm museum, and a nature station.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.explorekentuckylake.com/murray/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Wrather West Kentucky Museum, housed in the first building on Murray State’s campus, earned a spot on the National Register of Historic Places.",
